{
 "result": "lcs.py is built and verified: it reads two lines from stdin and prints the LCS length, using a bit-parallel algorithm (Hyyro/Crochemore) on Python big ints. Match masks are built with str.translate + int(..,2), each step is v=(v+(v&mk))|(v&~mk) with ~mk precomputed, and the result is masked to width only every 64 steps. Correctness against a reference DP: ref.py unit and CLI checks pass; 3000 random small cases (alphabets of 1 to 26 symbols plus unicode, K=1/3/64) pass, run against the identical lcs3.py copy before it was installed as lcs.py; a further random test on lcs.py showed 0 mismatches; 3000-char strings over alphabets of 2, 4 and 26 symbols match DP (2425, 1951, 955); and big.txt gives 1943, equal to big.exp. CLI edge cases behave correctly: CRLF line endings, missing second line, empty stdin, and unicode input. Speed: `python3 assay.py` (100 calls on a 3000x3000 pair, best of 5, after CLI correctness checks) measured 911.3, 915.3, 915.6 and 918.7 calls/s against the registered baseline of 553.5, about +65% and well beyond the 5% noise, or roughly 1.1 ms per 3000-char pair in-process. No `try` daughter was used; the gains came from in-place edits (skipping the per-step mask gave about +50%, precomputing the complement mask about +8%).",
